Installed `GlimmProvider` and `useGlimm` (`@/components/ui/glimm`), the framework-agnostic core (`glimm-core`, with `createShader`, `playSweep`, the palettes and easings) and the `render-loop` helper in `lib/`.

Wrap your app once, then call `sweep` from anywhere inside it:

```tsx
import { GlimmProvider, useGlimm } from "@/components/ui/glimm";

function NextButton({ onNext }: { onNext: () => void }) {
  const { sweep } = useGlimm();
  return <button onClick={() => sweep(onNext, { palette: "ember" })}>Next</button>;
}

<GlimmProvider palette="prism">
  <App />
</GlimmProvider>
```

`sweep(navigate, options?)` runs your navigation (a state change, `router.push`), waits for React to commit it, and reveals the new view behind the band. The browser holds the outgoing view with View Transitions; without them an opaque curtain covers it. To keep a transition inside one box, use `<GlimmProvider scope="container" className="h-64 rounded-xl">`: only that box is swept. Pass `focus: "h1"` to land focus on the new view's heading.

Drawing runs on the shared render loop: it pauses off screen and in hidden tabs, and under reduced motion (`reducedMotion`) or without WebGL a sweep swaps, fades or wipes instead.

Ported from glimm 0.3.1 by Noman Ijaz (MIT); the Next.js adapter is not included. The license text is in each file.
